The university's specialist campus for [[music]] and [[visual arts|visual]] and [[performing arts]] teaching is the [[3, Islington Square|Creative Campus]] in [[Everton, Liverpool|Everton]] next to [[St Francis Xavier Church, Liverpool|St Francis Xavier's Church]]. The university also has a residential-only campus, Aigburth Park in [[St Michael's Hamlet|St Michael's]], and Plas Caerdeon, an [[outdoor education]] centre in [[Snowdonia]], [[North Wales]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Liverpool Hope University|url=https://www.hope.ac.uk/lifeathope/ourcampuses|title=Our campuses|access-date=31 August 2020}}</ref>

The university's teaching campuses contain three [[Listed building#Grade II|Grade II listed building]]s. One of these is the former main building of Saint Katharine's College at Hope Park, now renamed as the Hilda Constance Allen Building.<ref name="Saint K">{{NHLE|num=1405046|desc=Former St Katharine's College|access-date=1 September 2020}}</ref> The Creative Campus includes the other two: the former Saint Francis Xavier's School (now the Cornerstone Building) designed by [[Henry Clutton]], and the former LSPCC (Liverpool Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children) building at 3 Islington Square.<ref>{{harvnb|Heery|2002|p=32}}</ref><ref>{{NHLE|num=1073440|desc=Saint Francis Xavier's Schools|access-date=1 September 2020|fewer-links=yes}}</ref><ref>{{NHLE|num=1075178|desc=3, Islington Square|access-date=4 September 2020|fewer-links=yes}}</ref>

Hope Park is [[bisection|bisected]] by Taggart Avenue, which runs north–south through the middle of the campus and divides the former sites of two of the university's three predecessor colleges. On the western side of Taggart Avenue is the former campus of Christ's College, while the eastern side (which besides Hilda Constance Allen also includes the EDEN Building and the Sheppard-Worlock Library) was formerly the campus of Saint Katharine's. In the era when the two colleges existed, high walls ran along both sides of Taggart Avenue, physically separating the institutions.<ref name="Ep7" />

The university's third predecessor college, Notre Dame, was located on [[Mount Pleasant, Liverpool|Mount Pleasant]] at its corner with Hope Street. Its former property, which it vacated in 1980, was acquired by Liverpool Polytechnic and became part of the campus of [[Liverpool John Moores University|LJMU]], the polytechnic's successor institution.<ref>{{harvnb|Elford|2003|pp=7–8}}</ref> Together with an adjoining [[townhouse]] it forms LJMU's John Foster Building.

==Organisation and administration==
The university follows a [[Usury#Christianity|Christian principle]] to avoid bank loans and has not taken out a new bank loan since the mid-2000s.<ref name="F2">{{Cite report|last=Liverpool Hope University|url=https://beta.companieshouse.gov.uk/company/03285547/filing-history|title=Full accounts made up to 31 July 2019|date=13 December 2019|pages=4{{hyphen}}6|access-date=7 September 2020}}</ref> Expenditure is financed from university cash reserves, and the university budget is set from zero each year with only permanent staffing rolled over.<ref name="F2" /> In 2018, the university established an Income Generation Plan to diversify income streams away from a reliance on [[Tuition fees in the United Kingdom#England|undergraduate tuition fees]].<ref name="F2" />

===Schools and departments===
The university comprises four faculties and nine schools.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Faculties |url=https://www.hope.ac.uk/studyathope/faculties/ |access-date=2024-11-30 |website=www.hope.ac.uk |language=en}}</ref> The School of Creative and Performing Arts is located at the Creative Campus, with all other schools/departments at Hope Park.

