===Branding===
[[File:Liverpool Hope crest.jpg|thumb|right|University coat of arms in a [[stained glass]] window at the southern end of the EDEN Building]]
Elford's ''The Foundation of Hope'' discusses how [[brand management]] was of particular importance to the university in the 1990s, with the inception of the "Hope [[brand]]" in 1995: "The Hope brand was vigorously developed and marketed"; "New [[Corporate identity#Corporate colours|corporate colours]] [were developed]".<ref>{{harvnb|Elford|2003|p=13}}</ref> The university had previously struggled to unite its three predecessor colleges into a single corporate identity, with "internal dissonances" persisting.<ref name="Ep12">{{harvnb|Elford|2003|p=12}}</ref> Elford argues that, during its time as Liverpool Institute of Higher Education, the university "had effectively failed to establish an identity of its own".<ref name="Ep12" />

The university adopted red as the main corporate colour of the Hope brand, primarily contrasted with white. It is the only university in the Liverpool metropolitan area that uses red, a corporate colour more commonly associated with universities elsewhere in the historic "[[Red Rose of Lancaster|red rose]]" county of Lancashire (in particular [[Lancaster University|Lancaster]], [[University of Salford|Salford]] and [[University of Lancashire|UCLan]]). The university uses red for spiritual/theological rather than geographical/historical reasons.<ref name="Fabrik">{{Cite web|last=Fabrik|url=https://fabrikbrands.com/portfolio/liverpool-hope-identity|title=Liverpool Hope branding|access-date=11 September 2020}}</ref> Its original (1995–2006) logo (the word "''hope''" written in red in lower case italics with the tail of the "e" turning upwards and encircling the word) can be found in ''The Foundation of Hope'' on the book's title page and rear cover.<ref>{{harvnb|Elford|2003}}</ref>

In 2016/17, the university began using its coat of arms as its sole corporate logo, emphasising its [[Brand management#Brand heritage|brand heritage]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Issuu|url=https://issuu.com/liverpoolhopeuniversity/docs|title=Liverpool Hope University|access-date=11 September 2020}}</ref> This involved retiring its most recent modern logo, which had been designed in partnership with the London-based [[advertising agency|creative agency]] Fabrik in 2006.<ref name="Fabrik" /> For the 175th anniversary, in 2019, the university presented its coat of arms alongside the legend "<small>175 YEARS OF ACADEMIC EXCELLENCE</small>".

==Academic profile==
In {{HESA year}}, the university had 305 academic staff.<ref name="HESA1">{{Cite web|last=HESA|url= https://www.hesa.ac.uk/data-and-analysis/staff/table-8|title=Higher education staff data – Table 8: HE academic staff by HE provider and highest qualification held|access-date=7 September 2020}}</ref> 230 of these (75.41%) were qualified to doctoral level, placing the university 16th highest in the UK on this measure.<ref name="HESA1" /><ref name="F1">{{Cite report|last=Liverpool Hope University|url=https://beta.companieshouse.gov.uk/company/03285547/filing-history|title= Full accounts made up to 31 July 2019|date=13 December 2019|page=3|access-date=7 September 2020}}</ref> The university's aim is for 85% of its academic staff to have doctorates and the remainder to be Professional Tutors with industry experience in areas such as education, law and accountancy.<ref name="F1" />

===Partnerships===
The university is a member of the [[Cathedrals Group]]. Within this [[Universities in the United Kingdom#Mission groups|mission group]], the university validates and awards the degrees of PhD and MPhil for [[Birmingham Newman University]] and [[St Mary's University, Twickenham]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Newman University|url=https://www.newman.ac.uk/knowledge-base/what-next|title=Research and higher degrees|access-date=25 August 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=St Mary's University, Twickenham|url=https://www.stmarys.ac.uk/research/degree-students/overview.aspx|title=Postgraduate research degrees|access-date=25 August 2020}}</ref>

The university has a number of international partnerships with other academic institutions, many of whom are [[Christian college|Christian universities]]. Major partners include [[Université catholique de Lille]] in France, [[Christ University]] and [[Stella Maris College, Chennai]] in India, two American liberal arts colleges [[Hope College]] and [[Ouachita Baptist University]], and [[Sun Yat-sen University]] in China.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Liverpool Hope University|url=https://www.hope.ac.uk/international/partnerships|title=Partnerships|access-date=25 August 2020}}</ref>

The university's Network of Hope was established in 1998 as a set of partnerships with [[Catholic Church|Catholic]] [[sixth form college|sixth-form colleges]] in the [[North West England|North West]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Liverpool Hope University|url=https://www.hope.ac.uk/media/aboutus/images/Timeline_Exhibition_Boards__07.pdf|title= The Liverpool Hope Story: Story of Hope Exhibition Board 7 – 1998|access-date=25 August 2020}}</ref> Current Network of Hope partners include [[Carmel College (St Helens)]], [[Holy Cross College (UK)|Holy Cross College]] in [[Bury, Greater Manchester|Bury]] and [[St John Rigby College, Wigan]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Liverpool Hope University|url=https://www.hope.ac.uk/undergraduate/networkofhope|title=Network of Hope|access-date=25 August 2020}}</ref> Its business school is an [[Association to Advance Collegiate Schools of Business|AACSB]] member.

===Everton F.C.===
In 2016 the university signed a five-year partnership agreement with [[Everton F.C.|Everton F.C]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Connelly|first=Tony|url=https://www.thedrum.com/news/2016/10/14/everton-fc-partner-with-liverpool-hope-university-research-push-fanbase-development|title=Everton FC partner with Liverpool Hope University for research push into fanbase development and commercial growth|date=14 October 2016|access-date=14 September 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=fcbusiness|url=https://fcbusiness.co.uk/news/everton-join-forces-with-liverpool-hope-university|title=Everton Football Club and Liverpool Hope University have announced plans for an official research partnership – the first of its type between a Premier League club and a university|access-date=14 September 2020}}</ref> The partnership included a monitoring and evaluation project on the club's Everton in the Community Free School (opened in 2011)<ref>{{cite news|title=Everton FC charity's free school|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-merseyside-15729296|access-date=31 August 2020|last=Hodges|first=Lucy|date=14 November 2011|work=[[BBC News]]}}</ref> and graduate scholarships to research the club's history.<ref>{{cite web|last=MadeAtUni|url=https://madeatuni.org.uk/liverpool-hope-university/measuring-social-impact-everton-fc|title=Measuring social impact of Everton FC|date=28 July 2020|access-date=14 September 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Carroll|first=Sam|url=https://www.liverpoolecho.co.uk/sport/football/football-news/everton-set-fund-history-scholarship-18366461|title= Everton set to fund history scholarship at Hope University that could involve new stadium transition|date=5 June 2020|access-date=14 September 2020}}</ref>

